# startup-notification -- the "application is loading" feedback protocol.
#
# HERE BECAUSE OF ONE LINE IN MUTTER, and the reasoning is worth recording
# because the obvious conclusion is the wrong one. mutter is built
# -Dx11=false in this tree -- there is no X server here and no native X11
# session is possible -- so it would be natural to assume the X11-era startup
# feedback goes with it. It does not:
#
#     mutter meson.build:138  have_x11_client = have_x11 or have_xwayland
#     mutter meson.build:329  have_startup_notification =
#                                 get_option('startup_notification') and have_x11_client
#
# have_xwayland is TRUE, so have_x11_client is TRUE, so this is still required.
# X applications running under Xwayland are exactly the ones whose startup
# feedback would otherwise be missing.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://www.freedesktop.org/software/startup-notification/releases/startup-notification-0.12.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=3c391f7e930c583095045cd2d10eb73a64f085c7fde9d260f2652c7cb3cfbe4a
SRC_DIR=startup-notification-0.12

# The complete requirement list, read from configure.in 81-88 rather than from
# the book. All four are hard -- each PKG_CHECK_MODULES has an AC_MSG_ERROR:
#
#     xcb >= 1.6   -> libxcb      packaged
#     xcb-aux      -> xcb-util    packaged alongside this
#     xcb-event    -> xcb-util    packaged alongside this
#     x11-xcb      -> libX11      packaged
#
# BLFS says only "Xorg Libraries and xcb-util", where "Xorg Libraries" is a
# CATEGORY rather than a list -- so it was worth confirming that libSM and
# libICE, which this tree does NOT package, are not among them. They are not.
#
# This recipe ships its own build.sh, which is unusual for a plain autotools
# package. The reason is in that file: the tarball is from 2011 and its
# config.guess predates aarch64, so configure must be told --build explicitly --
# and in the three-part form, because the equally old config.sub rejects the
# two-part triple that gcc -dumpmachine produces. Without it this package is
# green on x86_64 and red on aarch64, which reads like a flaky runner.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static"
